00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.620
And of course the shattered here

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:37.750
refers to the shattered windows
and doors and structures,

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.570
but also to the shattered humanity

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.783
that we all experienced at that point.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.990
My objectives here what
I'd like to share with you

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.280
and I am putting them in a form

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.593
of an unusually for objectives,
in form of question.

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:59.330
Because I'm still questioning myself

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:02.020
and I'd like you to
join me on this thinking

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:03.980
of what lens should we use

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:05.760
to analyze public health challenges?

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.190
And proposing a more or less

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.290
a different way of looking
at time, place, and person,

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:15.260
and I'll explain that as we move forward.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:18.350
Again, this presentation
is not going to be

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.190
about numerators and denominators

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.540
and really about prevalences.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.420
Few numbers here and there.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.650
It's going to be mostly focusing

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:30.520
on the social politic
and historical context,

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.270
actually more on the
political and historical

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:34.280
to just give a meaning

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:38.080
to why are we in the
situation that we're facing?

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:42.890
Not only in Lebanon and the
region, also across the globe.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.980
What lessons can we learn to
inform global public health?

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:49.690
And what can we do all together

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.753
perhaps to achieve a fairer world?

00:05:54.107 --> 00:05:56.333
I will start where you will not expect.

00:05:57.620 --> 00:06:02.153
I am going to define my place
as Minneapolis, Minnesota.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:06.133
My time as 25 May '22.

00:06:07.061 --> 00:06:10.463
And my person, so this is the place, time,

00:06:11.844 --> 00:06:14.840
and my person is George Floyd.

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.463
George Floyd is, was died
at the age of 46 years.

00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:22.190
He's a black American

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:26.000
and he was a victim of police brutality

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:29.570
for different reasons,
one of them you all know

00:06:29.570 --> 00:06:34.333
and that's this the color
of the skin that he carries.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.560
One of his last words

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:40.270
what he whispered before he passed away

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:42.560
was "I cannot breathe".

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:43.393
And.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.043
That incident.

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.280
Started a practically a wide
unrest and social movement

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:54.800
and political movement
across the United States

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:57.480
and I would say across
the nation divisions

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:59.440
and geographic divisions.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:03.040
All calling for justice
not only for George,

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.250
justice for the minorities,

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:07.770
the disadvantaged, the underprivileged.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.070
And I think there's an important lesson

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:11.873
for all of us.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.480
This was 2020.

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:18.250
I'm going to take you 10 years back

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:22.810
to Ben Arous on 17 December, 2010.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.640
Again place and time.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.183
Ben Arous is in Tunisia
and the person is Bouazizi.

00:07:30.074 --> 00:07:34.890
Mohamed Bouazizi was
26-years-old when he again

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:37.280
fell a victim to police brutality.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.310
He was unemployed,

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:42.690
trying to work what was considered
illegally on the street,

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:46.520
and in reaction he set himself on fire.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:51.343
His mom refers to this as an
act to defend his dignity.

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:56.680
That incident started
what everybody talks about

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.023
as the Arab uprising.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:03.920
That code for dignity, for
freedom, for liberation,

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:08.070
for a new world actually
swept the Arab world

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:11.480
and many many many countries
and capitals actually.

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:14.980
So thousands and thousand
and millions of people

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:16.040
taking to the street

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:22.710
demanding minimum
acceptable living conditions

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:24.173
and minimum dignity.

00:08:25.459 --> 00:08:29.570
I'll take you 10 years back
and again younger individual.

00:08:30.460 --> 00:08:35.460
Take you to Gaza on the
30th of September 2000.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:40.240
And the person in this
story is Muhammad al-Durrah.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.950
Muhammad was 12-years-old when
he was caught with his father

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:48.110
in a cross fire between
Palestine and the Israeli's

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:51.670
and he was shot by
Israeli soldiers and ended

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.530
and his father was wounded.

00:08:56.530 --> 00:09:00.170
This took place around the
practically within every day

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:05.170
or two of the start of the
second intifada in Palestine.

00:09:06.730 --> 00:09:09.730
And that extended over
more than four years.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:12.920
The pictures I chose here
I chose intentionally

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:17.420
to show you that this is a
struggle between civilians,

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:19.840
against young and old,

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.520
against one of the mightiest

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.303
military powers in the
region if not in the globe.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:31.490
I can't breathe.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:34.950
I would like to adopt that as a road map

00:09:34.950 --> 00:09:36.667
for global public health.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:39.440
I can't breathe

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:42.940
represents people who are
fighting racism and feeling

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:47.410
that there's structural and systemic

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:49.850
discrimination against them.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:54.780
I can't breathe is a scream
by people in our region

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.110
and most in many many
countries around the world

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:02.570
fighting against authoritarian
system, against corruption.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:06.270
And I can't breathe part is the voice

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.650
of people who are still under occupation

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:12.340
and colonian powers and under three cases

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:14.530
what global public health can do

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.640
is actually be part of this struggle

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.130
of this passage of this
journey from racism

00:10:22.130 --> 00:10:24.310
to a society

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.240
or to a world where people's identity

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.900
and self-realization is respected.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.060
To a world that moves
from authoritarianism

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:39.190
to perhaps countries where
people are held accountable for,

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.910
especially politicians,
for their misdoings,

00:10:41.910 --> 00:10:45.700
where they can live in dignity
and express themselves.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:48.930
Again a passage, a
journey helping people go

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:51.660
from being under occupation

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.960
to a world with liberty and freedom.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:10:57.192
If you think about this

00:10:57.192 --> 00:11:00.510
this is exactly what
we do in public health.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:04.100
In public health we talk about
community based movements,

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:08.180
we talk about solidarity,
we talk about action,

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:10.980
we talk about listening to the victims,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.823
we talk about listening to
our social participants.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:18.010
This is what I am calling for here

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.790
is to listen to people
saying I can't breathe.

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:23.630
I'm trying to work together with them

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:24.683
to a better future.

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:28.850
I think this sentence summarizes it all

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.070
and this is from the landmark report

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:34.940
of the Commission on Social
Determinants in 2008.

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:35.773
That after all,

00:11:35.773 --> 00:11:39.330
social injustice is the
main killer in our globe,

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.663
it's a killer on a grand scale.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:46.500
I take all of this to
sort of put it together

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:51.380
in a model of how I myself
look at public health

00:11:51.380 --> 00:11:52.830
and global public health.

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:57.474
And I see here three
determinant that actually.

00:11:57.474 --> 00:11:58.540
- [Man] That's crazy.

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:01.130
- Influence what we do.

00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:04.736
One is identity, how we as individuals,

00:12:04.736 --> 00:12:07.870
as human beings, how do we see ourselves?

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:12.430
How do we recognize our
identity and what we stand for?

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.950
But at the same time
this could be in contrast

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:18.590
and in conflict with how others see us.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:23.590
The identity perceived by us
and perceived by the other,

00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:25.800
that kind of conflict

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:29.300
determines the health of people
and priorities of people.

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:30.680
Social determinants of health

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:33.993
is known to all of if not most of us.

00:12:34.846 --> 00:12:37.860
It's about the occupation and finding,

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:41.890
it's about finding employment,
it's about education,

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:44.063
about these opportunities for better life.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:48.780
And but I put political
determinants of health

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:52.140
as an independent domain.

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:53.700
And I put it on purpose there.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:57.600
Because some people claim
and say that it is inclusive,

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:00.290
it is included under social determinants.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.120
I think we need to split it out,

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.670
we need to have it out there in our face

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:08.960
because when you talk about
political determinants of health

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:13.310
we're talking about governance,
accountability, corruption,

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:15.820
colonialism, structural violence,

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:17.300
this is what comes to our mind

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:18.430
when we talk about the term

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:19.840
political determinants of health.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:23.310
And it shouldn't be diluted by any way

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.780
by just mixing it up with the other term

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:28.270
of social determinants of health.

00:13:28.270 --> 00:13:30.690
And those three impact our health

00:13:30.690 --> 00:13:32.750
not only at the personal level

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.950
also at the collective
and population level.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:40.440
The Association of
Schools of Public Health,

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:44.350
schools and programs of
public health here in the U.S.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:46.570
in 2020 held a leadership retreat.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:49.110
This is an annual event, it was virtual.

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.290
The focus was on structural
and systemic racism.

00:13:52.290 --> 00:13:57.290
Of course this was driven
by George Floyd case.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:02.400
And one of the most
profound statements I heard

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.250
at the end of this panel,

00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:07.220
when the moderator asked
the deans and directors

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.940
what is one thing that you would do

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:13.343
in your school or program
addressing racism?

00:14:14.210 --> 00:14:17.250
And dean Thomas LaVeist,

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:19.420
the dean of the Tulane
School of Public Health

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.330
said we need to teach our
public health students

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:26.550
the history of the
United States of America.

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:28.650
For me this was a revelation.

00:14:28.650 --> 00:14:29.930
It's thought again

00:14:29.930 --> 00:14:33.300
speaks to many of the issues that we face

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:35.600
not only in our region, across the world,

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.970
that there's history
and there's a background

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:41.430
and there's a context for
everything that we're living in.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:43.740
And he has this movement that is a book

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:46.700
that is a documentary that speeches

00:14:46.700 --> 00:14:49.630
that he refers to as the skin you are in,

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:52.500
the skin you are in defines you.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.580
And I think this is again
an amazing statement,

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:58.490
because he didn't talk
about the color of the skin.

00:14:58.490 --> 00:14:59.670
The skin you're in,

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:03.230
because what's inside that
skin is a human being.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:07.880
And that goes anatomy and
pathology and physiology

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:11.103
and perhaps genetics are
more or less the same.

00:15:11.990 --> 00:15:16.200
What differs is that tiny thin envelope

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.320
that covers our human being-ness.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:21.970
And I think that's the
kind of public health

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:24.250
that we should be talking about.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:25.500
Crossing that barrier,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:30.500
crossing that skin, to look
at in depth at our humanity

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.343
and the human beings.

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:38.260
To add to these concepts
that I am introducing

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:41.653
I would like to introduce the
concept of wounds or traumas.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:43.610
And there are three

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:49.200
definitions or terms
that I'll use with this.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:51.373
One, the invisible wound.

00:15:51.373 --> 00:15:53.690
We used to talk about the visible wound,

00:15:53.690 --> 00:15:57.713
what we can see, what
the physician can stitch.

00:15:59.459 --> 00:16:01.540
But we are talking about invisible wounds

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:04.830
that we cannot see that are pushed inside.

00:16:04.830 --> 00:16:07.010
And our job as public health people,

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:08.320
our job as public health professionals

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.100
is to uncover the wounds inside,

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:13.020
these are the wounds that are caused

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:16.030
by discrimination, by injustice.

00:16:16.030 --> 00:16:17.663
They don't show out.

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:22.410
These wounds could
become intergenerational.

00:16:22.410 --> 00:16:25.870
There are now reports and
studies talking about slavery

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:29.460
and that it impacts
even our genetic makeup.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.150
These wounds are transferred,

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.300
and pains and sufferings are transferred

00:16:34.300 --> 00:16:35.840
from one generation to the other

00:16:35.840 --> 00:16:38.100
and we need to keep that in mind.

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:39.810
And then the value of wounds.

00:16:39.810 --> 00:16:41.160
It's not stable.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:42.730
We all know the narrative.

00:16:42.730 --> 00:16:44.640
We could know and draw the narrative

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.503
and how a wound was caused.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:49.090
Who killed who shot who,

00:16:49.090 --> 00:16:51.840
and who was belligerent,
and who was the victim.

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:54.780
But the value of the wound
and how you would perceive it

00:16:54.780 --> 00:16:57.099
is defined politically.

00:16:57.099 --> 00:17:00.930
Veterans in this country
of the Vietnam War

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:02.430
have been treated differently

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:07.420
than the veterans of the
Gulf War in the same country.

00:17:07.420 --> 00:17:10.380
And that's defined politically
with the political agenda

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:13.000
and the mood of the country.

00:17:13.000 --> 00:17:16.720
And these values and
how we see these wounds

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.150
internal and external differ with time.

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:22.150
To the left of the slide there is a key.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:26.330
And I think this is a key
that helps us understand

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:27.710
what these wounds are,

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:30.690
but also this key is a symbol.

00:17:30.690 --> 00:17:33.090
And maybe I can't talk about it at the end

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.820
or under Q and A.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:37.273
It is also a political symbol.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:41.430
Now I take you to Lebanon.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:45.720
And in my presentation I'm
going to zoom in and out

00:17:47.475 --> 00:17:49.410
and take you through this journey

00:17:49.410 --> 00:17:52.623
using this concept that I
presented as my guideline.

00:17:54.190 --> 00:17:58.860
In Beirut August four
2020 six or seven PM,

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:01.860
a time that nobody will forget,

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:05.070
this explosion took place.

00:18:05.070 --> 00:18:08.740
This explosion now we have
the time, we have the place,

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:10.780
but this time it was not a person,

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:12.890
this time it was a population,

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:16.310
it was a whole collective experience.

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:20.103
It destroyed a sizable
portion of the City of Beirut.

00:18:21.000 --> 00:18:23.573
It killed more than 200 people.

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:27.570
It destroyed tens of thousands of houses,

00:18:27.570 --> 00:18:29.823
some of them severely damaged.

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:34.550
It destroyed hospitals
and health care centers.

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:37.563
Three of these hospitals
were severely damaged.

00:18:38.900 --> 00:18:42.850
More than 6,000 injuries were reported.

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.880
They stormed and the emergency departments

00:18:45.880 --> 00:18:48.450
across the hospitals in
Beirut and surrounding

00:18:48.450 --> 00:18:51.130
towns and cities.

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:53.760
Not only on their own,
they came with friends,

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:55.410
they came with relatives,

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.890
they came with their life, loved ones.

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:00.270
Tens of thousands of people

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:02.800
were actually roaming
around the emergency rooms

00:19:02.800 --> 00:19:05.570
and around hospitals
and the City of Beirut

00:19:05.570 --> 00:19:07.090
around that time.

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:10.490
We were all worried about
COVID-19 transmission.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:14.150
There was no way to impose
any protective measure there.

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:19.150
Low and behold this is August four here

00:19:19.320 --> 00:19:23.740
and within weeks the
cases of COVID spread out.

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:28.480
If this is a lesson of
how politics and conflict

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:31.240
connect with health

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:34.233
I think this is one of
the striking examples.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.400
People would ask what's in that plume

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:44.890
and what kind of toxic
agents we were exposed to.

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:48.960
But in Lebanon at that time
not only people in Beirut,

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:53.430
I think the strongest and
most common phrase used

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:56.170
was I can't breathe.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:20:01.110
In Arabic (Iman speaks
Arabic), I am suffocating.

00:20:01.110 --> 00:20:04.240
People across the city
and across the country

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:08.090
were talking about suffocation
and ability to breathe

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:11.410
because of the smoke, because of the dust

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:14.150
that was associated with the destruction.

00:20:14.150 --> 00:20:18.030
But also because of this
feeling of total oppression,

00:20:18.030 --> 00:20:21.120
of feeling that this political system,

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.080
this situation has robbed us

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:26.810
of what has been left of our dignity.

00:20:26.810 --> 00:20:28.390
And again.

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:31.690
It connects to all
these individual stories

00:20:31.690 --> 00:20:33.240
that I shared at the beginning.

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:36.010
This is again a picture

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:38.360
where you can easily see the damage

00:20:38.360 --> 00:20:39.680
and you can easily

00:20:41.620 --> 00:20:46.103
observe the external, the visible wounds.

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:52.060
The invisible wounds here
within this lady and others

00:20:52.160 --> 00:20:55.850
are things that we need to
keep observing and monitoring

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:57.690
and this is what matters.

00:20:57.690 --> 00:20:59.690
Because when you treat the visible wound

00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:02.170
people are forgotten.

00:21:02.170 --> 00:21:06.860
We need to keep remembering
that these wounds run deep,

00:21:06.860 --> 00:21:10.180
run deep to impact people's feelings

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:11.920
and people's mental health.

00:21:11.920 --> 00:21:13.360
One study that was done

00:21:13.360 --> 00:21:15.709
at the American University Medical Center

00:21:15.709 --> 00:21:19.760
and our faculty jointly looked
at mental health disorders

00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:23.130
among children less than 18-years-old.

00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:28.130
And again anxiety was on the
rise, PTSD, and depression.

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:30.490
Another colleague from our faculty

00:21:30.490 --> 00:21:34.030
looked at injuries and has
started now a cohort of injuries.

00:21:34.030 --> 00:21:36.550
I'm working with a group across Lebanon

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:39.130
to try and understand the interaction

00:21:39.130 --> 00:21:40.590
between intermittent exposure,

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:42.663
health outcomes, and vulnerability.

00:21:43.570 --> 00:21:47.120
Just to let you know, none
of these things were funded.

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:49.330
The first two studies
were done with no funds,

00:21:49.330 --> 00:21:51.050
totally on voluntary basis.

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:56.050
We've been reaching out to
fund our third proposed study

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:57.860
for more than a year.

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:02.190
There's no money to
document what has happened

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:04.020
and this would be a missed opportunity,

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.870
not only for Lebanon, for
the whole world to learn

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:08.440
what can a blast like this,

00:22:08.440 --> 00:22:11.690
what can explosion like
this cause do to people

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:15.423
not only on the visible but
also on the invisible wounds.

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.230
Just to share with you the context,

00:22:20.230 --> 00:22:22.164
this blast was not an incident.

00:22:22.164 --> 00:22:24.570
It's not an accident.

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:27.000
This blast is contextualized,

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:30.570
it's part of a stream of
things that were happening

00:22:30.570 --> 00:22:32.340
in the country.

00:22:32.340 --> 00:22:34.430
We published a paper right after,

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:36.680
talking about the Beirut blast

00:22:36.680 --> 00:22:39.180
and the escalation of the crisis.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:41.520
At the heart of all of this

00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:45.580
is a confessional
sectarian political system.

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:47.720
At the heart of all of this

00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:50.890
is political and economic mismanagement.

00:22:50.890 --> 00:22:53.660
And when the two things exist together

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:54.860
like a country in Lebanon,

00:22:54.860 --> 00:22:57.650
you have the worst combination ever.

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:00.000
In a region that is afflicted

00:23:01.001 --> 00:23:03.023
by instability and wars.

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:08.350
The turning point, the history is long,

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:11.850
but a turning point was February 14th 2005

00:23:11.850 --> 00:23:14.873
when former Prime Minister
Rafic Hariri was assassinated.

00:23:15.710 --> 00:23:18.310
That split the country.

00:23:18.310 --> 00:23:21.260
And within less than a month

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:23.410
we started talking about in the country

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:27.420
referring to two political
coalitions on blocks in 2005,

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:30.370
the march eight and the march 14.

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:33.300
One with an orientation towards the east

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:36.790
and one with orientation towards the west.

00:23:36.790 --> 00:23:39.510
And that kind of polarization

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:42.260
impacted how the country
is being run and lead

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:44.361
to further deterioration

00:23:44.361 --> 00:23:46.970
in the management even
of the basic services

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:50.933
provided to the citizens or
the residents of the country.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:55.930
We actually witness one of the scandalous

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:59.180
unacceptable and unbelievable

00:23:59.180 --> 00:24:01.710
garbage crisis that any country could see.

00:24:01.710 --> 00:24:05.030
In 2015 the country was drowning

00:24:06.453 --> 00:24:08.090
in its garbage.

00:24:08.090 --> 00:24:09.963
What you see here is this river,

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:15.520
this river is of white
plastic bags with garbage.

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:17.950
And that's what you see at the shores

00:24:17.950 --> 00:24:20.170
and some of the towns and villages.

00:24:20.170 --> 00:24:21.590
That was another indicator

00:24:21.590 --> 00:24:24.910
of a failing of the governing system,

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:26.500
a failing of a political system,

00:24:26.500 --> 00:24:28.903
the failing practically of a state.

00:24:31.540 --> 00:24:33.470
Electricity was started experiencing

00:24:33.470 --> 00:24:37.270
even further electricity shortages

00:24:37.270 --> 00:24:40.320
and more frequent outages.

00:24:40.320 --> 00:24:42.950
This is why the way the headquarters

00:24:42.950 --> 00:24:45.210
for the electricity of Lebanon.

00:24:45.210 --> 00:24:47.340
And it tells you sort of is an indicator

00:24:47.340 --> 00:24:49.058
for how bad the situation is.

00:24:49.058 --> 00:24:52.460
And think public health,
think health outcome.

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:55.570
No electricity means
your medical equipment

00:24:56.612 --> 00:24:59.212
are not running and you
have to look for alternative

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:01.320
sources of power.

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:03.830
This means that your
food cannot be stored,

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:06.690
this means that if it is the bad weather

00:25:08.315 --> 00:25:11.550
you might succumb to the weather elements.

00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:14.450
And people reverted to alternatives.

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:17.350
And these neighborhood

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:21.610
diesel generators marched
in all over the country.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:24.470
With that came earth pollution

00:25:24.470 --> 00:25:26.060
and the fact that people

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:31.060
and a study was run by
colleagues at the university.

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:34.100
What it showed what
when you turned them off

00:25:34.100 --> 00:25:35.271
and you turn them on,

00:25:35.271 --> 00:25:38.970
practically the particulate
matters and other toxins

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.280
sort of go through the roof.

00:25:43.170 --> 00:25:45.070
This lead to social movements.

00:25:45.070 --> 00:25:48.680
These social movements were
social slash environmental,

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:53.680
slash political, because they
followed the garbage crisis.

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:55.660
And there were demands,

00:25:55.660 --> 00:25:58.500
part of it technical,
part of it social reform,

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:01.150
and part of it political reform.

00:26:01.150 --> 00:26:02.190
And this is where

00:26:03.300 --> 00:26:07.990
health, politics, social
issues actually come together.

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:10.440
And it's very difficult in many cases

00:26:10.440 --> 00:26:12.620
to separate them from each other.

00:26:12.620 --> 00:26:14.090
There was a hope there

00:26:14.090 --> 00:26:16.810
that perhaps the social
these social movements

00:26:16.810 --> 00:26:21.810
political movements, fresh young
people perhaps could impact

00:26:21.860 --> 00:26:25.170
the election the
parliamentary election 2018.

00:26:25.170 --> 00:26:26.349
It didn't happen.

00:26:26.349 --> 00:26:28.570
It didn't happen because again

00:26:28.570 --> 00:26:30.920
this is sort of a quick context,

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:33.560
because the whole electric door

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:36.890
is based on sectarian divisions.

00:26:36.890 --> 00:26:38.740
So this is the District of Beirut

00:26:38.740 --> 00:26:40.910
one and we have eight seats.

00:26:40.910 --> 00:26:43.590
Three are reserved to
an Armenian Orthodox.

00:26:43.590 --> 00:26:45.940
One is reserved to an Armenian Catholic,

00:26:45.940 --> 00:26:48.800
one for a Modernite, one for a Catholic,

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:51.220
one for an Orthodox,
and one for a minority.

00:26:51.220 --> 00:26:52.970
You go to Beirut two,

00:26:52.970 --> 00:26:56.710
you have six for Sunni, two
for Shia's, one for Druze,

00:26:56.710 --> 00:27:00.500
and so on so forth in every
single district in the country.

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:02.850
What does that mean?

00:27:02.850 --> 00:27:06.430
That if you are a non
sectarian, if you are secular,

00:27:06.430 --> 00:27:09.030
regardless of what geology you carry,

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:11.130
if you want to change the system

00:27:11.130 --> 00:27:14.350
you are forced whether
you run for election

00:27:14.350 --> 00:27:18.380
or you vote on the election
that your voice counts

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:21.040
and you have to follow
the sectarian divisions,

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:24.403
acknowledge the sectarian
division in the country.

00:27:26.230 --> 00:27:28.760
Things got started getting worse and worse

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:29.960
on October 17.

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:33.430
One of the them came up
with an unpopular decision

00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:36.730
and for a reason that no one understands

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:39.490
tens and hundreds and thousands of people

00:27:39.490 --> 00:27:40.350
took to the street.

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.770
Within days there was an uprising,

00:27:43.770 --> 00:27:47.510
people refer to it as October
17 revolution in 2019.

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:51.540
People all over the country,
every city, every town,

00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:55.810
every village, went down
there and said it has to stop,

00:27:55.810 --> 00:27:57.220
things have to stop.

00:27:57.220 --> 00:28:00.620
People should be held accountable
and things need to change.

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:05.620
There was an amazing actually
expression of solidarity

00:28:05.650 --> 00:28:09.360
when the Lebanese and others joined hands

00:28:09.360 --> 00:28:11.990
from the North of Lebanon
to the South of Lebanon

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:15.670
it was a human chain of
tens of thousands of people.

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:17.560
It was interrupted in some places.

00:28:17.560 --> 00:28:20.060
Even that interruption the human chain

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:23.383
had a political connotation
and political explanation.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.850
It was peaceful but people were angry

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:32.300
and demanding accountability

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:34.795
and of course the response was brutal

00:28:34.795 --> 00:28:38.833
and they were attacked and
taken to prison and injured.

00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:46.690
Around the time that
October 17 uprising started

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:50.460
the banks closed its
doors for two three weeks.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:53.900
And when they opened all of us,

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:54.733
Lebanese, non Lebanese,

00:28:54.733 --> 00:28:56.770
anyone who has an account in the bank

00:28:56.770 --> 00:29:00.160
we had no access to all our
money, the door was closed.

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:03.943
From there on the banks gave us a stipend.

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:06.790
They would decide on how much money

00:29:06.790 --> 00:29:08.970
we can take from our saving account

00:29:08.970 --> 00:29:11.980
or our current account in Lebanese lira's,

00:29:11.980 --> 00:29:14.193
in dollars, or whatever other currency.

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:19.080
This started the Lebanese pound,

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:20.903
Lebanese currency collapsed.

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:27.800
For 30 years it had it was
pegged at 1,500 lira's,

00:29:28.690 --> 00:29:31.270
the dollar, and quickly
over the last three years

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:33.830
you see it's currently I think today

00:29:33.830 --> 00:29:37.853
it's close to 30,000 lira's per dollar.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:39.850
What does this mean?

00:29:39.850 --> 00:29:41.700
Just to give you an illustration.

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:45.047
So if your monthly salary
was 1,500,000 lira's

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:49.833
at the end of 2019 that
was 1,000 dollars sadly.

00:29:50.770 --> 00:29:54.090
Acceptably, decent, not great but decent.

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:58.170
Now this same salary is 60 dollars.

00:29:58.170 --> 00:30:00.660
On top of it you have
no access to your money

00:30:00.660 --> 00:30:03.183
if you have any in the banks,

00:30:03.183 --> 00:30:06.265
which means that the purchasing power

00:30:06.265 --> 00:30:08.970
of the Lebanese totally collapsed,

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:13.160
lost more than 90 to 95% of its value.

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:15.370
High inflation in the country

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:18.690
and lot of frustration on top of that.

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:21.050
People depend on emergencies,

00:30:21.050 --> 00:30:24.590
their loved ones outside
Lebanon sending some dollars,

00:30:24.590 --> 00:30:27.190
and of course humanitarian
and financial aid

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:29.853
from agencies, international and national.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:32.770
And then COVID-19

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:34.610
in February 2020.

00:30:34.610 --> 00:30:36.470
I'm not going talk about COVID-19

00:30:36.470 --> 00:30:39.310
and I'm not going to talk
about the response to COVID-19.

00:30:39.310 --> 00:30:42.040
I'm just going to say
that COVID-19 came here

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:44.260
and what it did politically

00:30:44.260 --> 00:30:47.470
it stopped all profit in the street.

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:49.057
Everybody went home.

00:30:49.057 --> 00:30:51.760
There was lockdown, people went home,

00:30:51.760 --> 00:30:54.310
and every single expression

00:30:54.310 --> 00:30:58.740
of protest were angered
went away because people

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:02.080
were worried about their
health, their loved ones,

00:31:02.080 --> 00:31:03.880
and they were taking this pandemic

00:31:03.880 --> 00:31:06.253
as they should, very seriously.

00:31:07.990 --> 00:31:12.990
The European report
summarizes all in this quote.

00:31:13.127 --> 00:31:15.937
"Decades of political and
financial mismanagement

00:31:15.937 --> 00:31:19.327
"combined with COVID-19
and the August 2020

00:31:19.327 --> 00:31:21.587
"Port of Beirut explosion

00:31:21.587 --> 00:31:24.627
"have brought the country to its knees."

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:31.130
There were bank stipulates
that the Lebanese crisis

00:31:31.130 --> 00:31:33.630
it could be possibly among the top three

00:31:33.630 --> 00:31:36.710
most severe crisis episodes globally

00:31:36.710 --> 00:31:39.310
since the mid nineteenth century.

00:31:39.310 --> 00:31:41.633
This is a record for the country.

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:46.730
This situation again according
to this European report,

00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:51.580
poverty struck across the
population, 74% of population.

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:56.200
And a sort of poverty considered
under the line of poverty.

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:58.900
Syrian refugees, 90% in extreme poverty.

00:31:58.900 --> 00:32:02.150
Most people receive two
hours of electricity per day.

00:32:02.150 --> 00:32:05.210
The healthcare sector
is at breaking point.

00:32:05.210 --> 00:32:07.250
There's an exodus of health professionals,

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:10.670
nurses, physicians, name it, and others.

00:32:10.670 --> 00:32:15.110
And there's a shortage of
medicine and medical supplies.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:17.570
There's an issue, a very
basic economic issue.

00:32:17.570 --> 00:32:21.660
What can you use to charge
people and to buy supplies?

00:32:21.660 --> 00:32:23.020
They are two different currencies

00:32:23.020 --> 00:32:24.700
that now don't talk to each other

00:32:24.700 --> 00:32:27.423
and that are not controlled.

00:32:28.310 --> 00:32:30.400
And to top it all

00:32:30.400 --> 00:32:34.943
they expect that the economy
will further shrink in 2022.

00:32:36.220 --> 00:32:38.880
Again another addition
with the Ukraine war

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:40.610
we all, I didn't know,

00:32:40.610 --> 00:32:44.960
but I didn't know that close
to 80% of the wheat in Lebanon

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:47.080
is actually imported from Ukraine.

00:32:47.080 --> 00:32:49.480
So this adds also to the challenge

00:32:49.480 --> 00:32:52.073
of even putting bread on the table.

00:32:54.480 --> 00:32:57.877
Of course I did not forget the
refugees because the refugees

00:32:57.877 --> 00:33:00.980
are another dimension within the country

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:02.873
that we need to address.

00:33:04.210 --> 00:33:07.880
In Lebanon there is more than
one million Syrian refugees

00:33:07.880 --> 00:33:12.420
that started coming influx-ing
and coming to Lebanon

00:33:12.420 --> 00:33:15.713
as the war in Syria started in 2011.

00:33:18.761 --> 00:33:22.450
And on top of that we talk
about close to 300,000

00:33:22.450 --> 00:33:26.053
Palestinian refugees with us for decades.

00:33:28.450 --> 00:33:29.283
This.

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:34.450
Report from Brookings talk
about the Syrian refugees

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:36.490
being a crisis within a crisis.

00:33:36.490 --> 00:33:39.760
I would say this is a
crisis on top of a crisis,

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:42.830
a crisis interacting with a crisis.

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:47.830
And Syrian's, most of them
have no legal residency,

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:52.850
they're 50% suffer from
insecurity food insecurity

00:33:52.850 --> 00:33:55.370
that doubled since 2019.

00:33:55.370 --> 00:33:59.860
90% live in extreme poverty,
almost double since 2019.

00:33:59.860 --> 00:34:03.093
Situation is not much
different for the Lebanese.

00:34:04.360 --> 00:34:05.720
And the country also,

00:34:05.720 --> 00:34:08.450
this is a point that
I will refer to later,

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:10.950
the country also when you
look at the healthcare systems

00:34:10.950 --> 00:34:13.350
there are multiple healthcare systems

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:16.450
that address the needs
of different populations.

00:34:16.450 --> 00:34:19.386
UNHCR, these were the refugees.

00:34:19.386 --> 00:34:21.905
UNRWA these were the Palestinian's.

00:34:21.905 --> 00:34:22.980
And then with Lebanese,

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:25.650
depending whether you have
private insurance or not,

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:28.110
is the Ministry of Health
or Social Insurance

00:34:28.110 --> 00:34:29.260
that takes care of you.

00:34:31.800 --> 00:34:34.730
But we don't live in isolation,
we're part of a region,

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:37.520
and we need to understand
a little bit more

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:42.333
how the region is impacting
us and impacting itself.

00:34:43.400 --> 00:34:45.690
This is the Arab world that we define

00:34:45.690 --> 00:34:49.010
as 22 states that are numbers

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:50.723
of the league of Arab states.

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:54.340
It's not a homogenous region.

00:34:54.340 --> 00:34:57.990
It's quite heterogeneous, a
lot of inequities actually.

00:34:57.990 --> 00:35:02.220
It comprises some of the
wealthiest countries in the world

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:05.310
and also some of the poorest
countries in the world.

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:06.822
I'm going to focus on two issues

00:35:06.822 --> 00:35:10.320
that define the region big time.

00:35:10.320 --> 00:35:11.663
One of them is conflicts.

00:35:12.800 --> 00:35:17.800
Close to 50% of the Arab
countries are in some kind of

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:21.020
some sort of phase of
political instability

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:24.033
or an upend war and conflict.

00:35:25.250 --> 00:35:26.500
What has changed?

00:35:26.500 --> 00:35:29.240
There were wars before
the Palestinian issue

00:35:29.240 --> 00:35:32.150
has been with us for more than 80 years.

00:35:32.150 --> 00:35:34.334
We had the civil war in Lebanon '75,

00:35:34.334 --> 00:35:36.210
1975 to 1990.

00:35:36.210 --> 00:35:39.073
There was the Iraq War,
the Gulf one, Gulf two,

00:35:40.290 --> 00:35:42.400
and so you had these wars here and there,

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:44.237
what has changed now in the region

00:35:44.237 --> 00:35:48.410
that you have these multiple
and concurrent conflicts

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:50.483
at the same time?

00:35:51.500 --> 00:35:55.193
They are protracted,
they're no more quick wars.

00:35:56.340 --> 00:36:01.340
And I would say that the
situation now in the region

00:36:01.400 --> 00:36:04.490
and also globally with these conflicts,

00:36:04.490 --> 00:36:06.570
that this idea previous idea

00:36:06.570 --> 00:36:11.500
that there's a pre conflict
phase, during conflict phase,

00:36:11.500 --> 00:36:14.340
and after conflict phase I think is that.

00:36:14.340 --> 00:36:16.970
That's the end of it, we
should declare this dead.

00:36:16.970 --> 00:36:20.260
You can no more divide conflicts
into these three phases.

00:36:20.260 --> 00:36:22.130
They are mingled together.

00:36:22.130 --> 00:36:25.160
Even if a war stops the
conflict doesn't stop.

00:36:25.160 --> 00:36:27.920
And the fact that they are
concurrent and multiple

00:36:27.920 --> 00:36:29.130
they're speaking to each other.

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:31.770
What's pre is post for others.

00:36:31.770 --> 00:36:34.530
And again the high number of IDP's,

00:36:34.530 --> 00:36:37.000
internal displaced people and refugees,

00:36:37.000 --> 00:36:38.870
the highest in the world.

00:36:38.870 --> 00:36:42.640
National borders are being
broken down within the country

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:44.353
and across the region.

00:36:44.353 --> 00:36:48.150
Anda again these wars are no
more national and local wars,

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:51.093
they are regionalized and
they are internationalized.

00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:56.010
One thing that was striking in these wars

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:57.600
that are happening now in the region

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:00.870
and unfortunately across other conflicts

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:02.950
is the weapon-ization of health,

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:06.570
using health as the tool of war.

00:37:06.570 --> 00:37:09.100
It happened strikingly in Syria.

00:37:09.100 --> 00:37:12.220
The Syrian government and
its allies were clearly

00:37:13.486 --> 00:37:17.374
attacking hospitals
and healthcare workers.

00:37:17.374 --> 00:37:19.352
And this is happening in Yemen,

00:37:19.352 --> 00:37:21.920
so the Arabia attacking
healthcare centers.

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:23.320
It happened in Afghanistan,

00:37:23.320 --> 00:37:27.110
even the U.S. has attacked health centers

00:37:27.110 --> 00:37:31.330
whether they claim it was
not on purpose or not,

00:37:31.330 --> 00:37:34.870
the fact that healthcare facilities

00:37:35.712 --> 00:37:36.860
are becoming a target

00:37:36.860 --> 00:37:38.913
and now we're seeing it also in Ukraine.

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:42.260
This is what I was talking about

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:45.420
about these huge numbers of population

00:37:45.420 --> 00:37:49.690
that are shifting within these
countries, breaking borders.

00:37:49.690 --> 00:37:51.940
You have Syrian's in Turkey
more than three million,

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:54.290
you have Syrian's in Lebanon,
more than one million.

00:37:54.290 --> 00:37:57.750
You have six million Syrian's
that have been shifted,

00:37:57.750 --> 00:38:00.020
displaced, within the country itself.

00:38:00.020 --> 00:38:04.250
Jordan, name it, there's
the conceptualization

00:38:04.250 --> 00:38:08.850
of a national border for a
conflict is disappearing.

00:38:08.850 --> 00:38:11.730
Our colleague Almara Diwachi

00:38:11.730 --> 00:38:14.150
and his co-authors and colleagues

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:15.642
have introduced this concept

00:38:15.642 --> 00:38:19.510
that referred to as
therapeutic geographies

00:38:19.510 --> 00:38:22.726
which they define as the
reorganization of healthcare

00:38:22.726 --> 00:38:26.830
within and across borders
because of these conditions.

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:28.430
A Syrian refugee in Turkey

00:38:28.430 --> 00:38:31.120
is receiving a totally different care

00:38:31.120 --> 00:38:35.210
than a Syrian refugee in
Jordan or Iraq or Lebanon

00:38:35.210 --> 00:38:37.073
or if they've gone to Europe.

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:40.370
And within the same country

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:43.750
they could be receiving
also a different care.

00:38:43.750 --> 00:38:46.490
You can no more talk about
national healthcare system

00:38:46.490 --> 00:38:48.523
and consistent health system.

00:38:49.720 --> 00:38:52.190
My dear friend and colleague Fouad Fouad

00:38:52.190 --> 00:38:53.720
talks about these dichotomy's

00:38:53.720 --> 00:38:56.950
that we need to start thinking about.

00:38:56.950 --> 00:39:00.560
The conflict phase
versus the post conflict.

00:39:00.560 --> 00:39:02.172
Are we dealing with emergencies

00:39:02.172 --> 00:39:04.360
or should we think long-term?

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:05.250
Are we dealing

00:39:05.250 --> 00:39:08.560
with acute illnesses and
health problems or chronic?

00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:09.670
Remember these refugees

00:39:09.670 --> 00:39:12.050
are mostly from middle income countries.

00:39:12.050 --> 00:39:15.970
We're talking cancer, chronic
diabetes, heart disease,

00:39:15.970 --> 00:39:17.430
we're talking injuries.

00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:19.699
We're not talking about diarrhea,

00:39:19.699 --> 00:39:21.363
and sanitation wholly.

00:39:22.680 --> 00:39:26.370
We're healthcare systems, we
need to address this issue.

00:39:26.370 --> 00:39:28.340
Are we running parallel
healthcare systems?

00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:32.260
How can these systems talk
to each other and integrate?

00:39:32.260 --> 00:39:35.610
Are we do we limit our
work to humanitarian

00:39:35.610 --> 00:39:37.294
and then think of development later,

00:39:37.294 --> 00:39:40.930
or should they converse with
each other at this point?

00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:44.330
And again who is our target,
who is our population?

00:39:44.330 --> 00:39:45.710
Is it refugees?

00:39:45.710 --> 00:39:46.960
Citizens?

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:50.020
Who takes priority and
who deals with whom?

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:53.580
These are really very striking dichotomy's

00:39:53.580 --> 00:39:56.110
that define not only the health outcomes,

00:39:56.110 --> 00:39:58.783
also the health system and the priorities.

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:01.550
El Norte.

00:40:01.550 --> 00:40:03.770
People are moving where?

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:07.560
Migrants, whether because
of environmental migration

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:11.610
or conflict Saharan Africa, North Africa,

00:40:11.610 --> 00:40:16.170
all our region, everybody is moving north

00:40:16.170 --> 00:40:18.630
for more stability and for the

00:40:19.870 --> 00:40:23.220
hope for a better life
and more stable life.

00:40:25.740 --> 00:40:29.113
The other issue in the Arab world besides.

00:40:32.167 --> 00:40:33.000
The.

00:40:34.280 --> 00:40:37.790
Conflicts is the issue of
ecological sustainability

00:40:37.790 --> 00:40:42.040
which is actually threatening
the survival of the region.

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:43.630
I'm not going to spend time here

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:45.800
but I am going to share numbers.

00:40:45.800 --> 00:40:48.200
If our population today in the Arab world

00:40:48.200 --> 00:40:52.050
is going to double or
increase by 50% if not double

00:40:52.050 --> 00:40:56.250
from 350 people are seeing
to 500 or 600 million

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:57.530
in 50 years.

00:40:57.530 --> 00:41:00.650
And if most of this
shift is in urbanization

00:41:00.650 --> 00:41:03.160
and larger and larger epicenters.

00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:06.840
And if this region is the most stressed.

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:08.630
You see the red here,

00:41:08.630 --> 00:41:12.880
this region is the most
stressed in water availability.

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:15.840
And it's expected if all these scenarios

00:41:15.840 --> 00:41:18.020
for climate change are correct

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:21.173
that we'll be he first region
to run out of fresh water.

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:25.410
And if this continues that we are

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:28.000
the largest importers
of cereal in the world

00:41:28.000 --> 00:41:31.400
Because we have added and semi arid lands.

00:41:31.400 --> 00:41:33.300
You put all of these together

00:41:33.300 --> 00:41:36.670
and you could really see what could happen

00:41:36.670 --> 00:41:40.660
in the region in 20,
30, 40 or 50 years time.

00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:44.330
And if these predictions and
projections with climb change

00:41:44.330 --> 00:41:48.220
then our region will be
the hottest in the globe

00:41:48.220 --> 00:41:51.030
and the least inhabitable in the globe.

00:41:51.030 --> 00:41:53.730
You're talking about
migrations in this case

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:57.270
environmental on top of
conflict and economic.

00:41:57.270 --> 00:42:00.003
And you can size it up in your mind.

00:42:02.701 --> 00:42:07.701
The DPM of WHO EMRO speaks
about the challenges

00:42:07.770 --> 00:42:11.350
to actually achieve
SDG's, weak governance,

00:42:11.350 --> 00:42:14.290
fragmented healthcare, limited data,

00:42:14.290 --> 00:42:15.890
the emergencies and the conflicts,

00:42:15.890 --> 00:42:19.993
and of course disparities in equalities.

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:23.190
I'm going to throw in
a piece of history here

00:42:23.190 --> 00:42:24.650
for the region.

00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:27.973
And of course this is
a personal perspective.

00:42:29.190 --> 00:42:33.570
I think of three dates that
are extremely important.

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:34.673
1918.

00:42:35.720 --> 00:42:38.500
This is when World War one ended

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:41.510
and there was lot of
aspiration in the Arab region,

00:42:41.510 --> 00:42:44.430
especially in the Levant,

00:42:44.430 --> 00:42:49.430
sort of the near Middle East,
the western Middle East.

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:53.880
And there was a hope of an
Arab kingdom of independence

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:57.500
of self-governance, but again it turns out

00:42:57.500 --> 00:42:59.620
that the British and the French empire

00:42:59.620 --> 00:43:01.780
decided to split that region,

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:05.040
create nation states that
were put under mandate,

00:43:05.040 --> 00:43:09.003
a nicer word for
colonialism, for 30 years.

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:12.930
1938 is another important date,

00:43:12.930 --> 00:43:15.230
and that's the time and the year

00:43:15.230 --> 00:43:17.750
when oil was discovered in Saudi Arabia.

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:21.810
That transform the region
into becoming a very sensitive

00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:26.240
and important geopolitical area.

00:43:26.240 --> 00:43:28.350
And it clings to the whole question

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:30.700
of energy and sources of energy.

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:32.580
And of course 1948.

00:43:32.580 --> 00:43:37.580
Because in 1948 again if you
look at it that year presents

00:43:38.060 --> 00:43:43.060
the birth of a new state in
the region the State of Israel

00:43:44.270 --> 00:43:46.860
versus the fact that for the Palestinian's

00:43:46.860 --> 00:43:49.610
and the Arab world it is referred to

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:51.630
as the catastrophe and (Iman
speaks foreign language).

00:43:51.630 --> 00:43:54.490
It is the time when hundreds
of thousands of Palestinian's

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:57.300
were uprooted and forced out.

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:00.710
And that still defines our relationships,

00:44:00.710 --> 00:44:04.060
still defines the politics,
still even defines

00:44:04.060 --> 00:44:08.760
these what we refer to
as regional coalitions.

00:44:08.760 --> 00:44:09.593
And orientation.

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:16.840
For those who still think
that I'm over claiming things

00:44:18.790 --> 00:44:21.870
this line here in the Arab world

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:25.730
to the north of this line you find Iran,

00:44:25.730 --> 00:44:28.690
Iraq, Syria, and Lebanon.

00:44:28.690 --> 00:44:30.620
This is one axis.

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:34.140
This is an axis that is looking east.

00:44:34.140 --> 00:44:36.000
Now in Lebanon there is a division,

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:39.670
and maybe that's where
some of the problems are.

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:43.430
And to the south of this
line you have Saudi Arabia,

00:44:43.430 --> 00:44:46.670
Jordan, Egypt, that are looking west.

00:44:46.670 --> 00:44:49.590
You'll find that most
of these countries now

00:44:49.590 --> 00:44:53.460
are starting negotiating
and a cause with Israel.

00:44:53.460 --> 00:44:56.010
And again it does define

00:44:56.010 --> 00:44:58.670
the politics and the social structure

00:44:58.670 --> 00:45:01.060
and stability or
instability in the region.

00:45:01.060 --> 00:45:06.060
This is Turkey again
interfering in Syria and Libia.

00:45:06.120 --> 00:45:09.560
And this is sort of the
regional-ization of the nation.

00:45:09.560 --> 00:45:12.100
This blue arrow here

00:45:13.290 --> 00:45:14.313
is the Nile River.

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:19.130
And this Nile River again
linking to water scarcity

00:45:19.130 --> 00:45:22.340
and link it to climate change,

00:45:22.340 --> 00:45:23.670
we know that what's happening now

00:45:23.670 --> 00:45:27.040
with Egypt, Sudan, and Ethiopia
and the dam in Ethiopia,

00:45:27.040 --> 00:45:29.650
this is a recipe for future conflict

00:45:29.650 --> 00:45:31.891
whether we like it or not.

00:45:31.891 --> 00:45:36.290
And again this is where
climate change, migration,

00:45:36.290 --> 00:45:38.593
conflict, politics, come together.

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:42.010
And then shouldn't be surprised

00:45:42.010 --> 00:45:45.900
why the Arab world is the spends more

00:45:45.900 --> 00:45:47.420
on military than health.

00:45:47.420 --> 00:45:50.343
It's the only such region
in the whole world.

00:45:51.880 --> 00:45:53.810
Moving out to the globe.

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:56.150
We're talking now about 84 million

00:45:56.150 --> 00:45:57.750
forcibly displaced people,

00:45:57.750 --> 00:46:01.590
I'm sorry about that,
this was November 2021.

00:46:01.590 --> 00:46:02.430
Because now we're talking

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:05.550
about an extra seven million Ukrainian's

00:46:05.550 --> 00:46:07.800
that have been added to this statistic.

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:11.700
And this number keeps
increasing year after year.

00:46:11.700 --> 00:46:14.500
That tells you that there
is a crisis in humanity

00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:16.790
and there is a crisis in our world.

00:46:16.790 --> 00:46:21.713
And it's not necessarily limited
to one region or the other.

00:46:21.713 --> 00:46:24.270
Some of the victims of
the sometimes forgot

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:27.160
are the young, the children and the youth,

00:46:27.160 --> 00:46:29.920
what we refer to as the lost generation.

00:46:29.920 --> 00:46:33.210
And you can also build
up with your imagination,

00:46:33.210 --> 00:46:36.780
what does it mean when you
have your full generation

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:39.320
that lost whole, that was displaced,

00:46:39.320 --> 00:46:41.560
didn't go to school, starved?

00:46:41.560 --> 00:46:43.640
What kind of future they have

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:46.323
and how can they rebuild their countries?

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:49.680
Coming at the end to hope.

00:46:49.680 --> 00:46:51.270
I have ideas here

00:46:51.270 --> 00:46:55.550
but because of time I'm going to stop here

00:46:55.550 --> 00:46:59.400
but share only one slide
and say I see a lot of hope

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:01.030
in the young generation.

00:47:01.030 --> 00:47:03.990
We have handed over to
the young generation

00:47:03.990 --> 00:47:06.440
a world in a total mess.

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:08.967
And we are counting on this new generation

00:47:08.967 --> 00:47:12.800
perhaps to do a better
job than our generation.

00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:14.323
And perhaps.

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:19.380
Try to introduce real change.

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:21.250
We have ideas for hope and change

00:47:21.250 --> 00:47:25.761
at the level of the community
and civil movements.

00:47:25.761 --> 00:47:29.400
The academic institutions have a role

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:32.860
and definitely there's important direction

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:36.070
that we should take and
that's global solidarity.

00:47:36.070 --> 00:47:40.410
With that I think our hopes winning.

00:47:40.410 --> 00:47:43.720
It's still in our hands
but we call the shots.

00:47:43.720 --> 00:47:46.110
I don't want to take more time

00:47:46.110 --> 00:47:50.900
to leave time for questions
and some interaction.

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:51.733
Thank you.

00:47:52.656 --> 00:47:55.020
- Doctor Iman thank you so much

00:47:55.020 --> 00:47:57.240
for this unbelievable presentation.

00:47:57.240 --> 00:47:59.610
It made me very emotional I must say.

00:47:59.610 --> 00:48:03.730
Thank you for that final
slide, the hope slide,

00:48:03.730 --> 00:48:07.260
because it again it
really made me emotional

00:48:07.260 --> 00:48:10.280
and also reminded me why many of us

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:12.580
got interested in public health.

00:48:12.580 --> 00:48:14.690
And as you made these

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:18.700
this amazing presentation
it's really making me think

00:48:18.700 --> 00:48:21.910
about how can we prepare our students,

00:48:21.910 --> 00:48:25.510
the next kind of the future
leaders in public health

00:48:26.950 --> 00:48:29.660
to be far better prepared than we are

00:48:29.660 --> 00:48:32.160
in responding to armed conflict,

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:35.879
to preventing these
political determinants of it?

00:48:35.879 --> 00:48:38.480
Because frankly you were bringing up

00:48:38.480 --> 00:48:40.210
the importance of
history of public health,

00:48:40.210 --> 00:48:43.293
which I completely agree,
values of public health.

00:48:45.620 --> 00:48:49.340
Honestly I don't think we
are spending enough time

00:48:50.620 --> 00:48:52.930
addressing those issues in our curriculum.

00:48:52.930 --> 00:48:57.620
So I'm just thinking, has the
American University of Beirut

00:48:57.620 --> 00:48:59.080
faculty health sciences,

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:02.750
have they redesigned their
public health curriculum

00:49:02.750 --> 00:49:04.530
to better prepare

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:08.100
the next generation of
students to understand?

00:49:08.100 --> 00:49:10.690
Because armed conflict
is kind of a new thing

00:49:10.690 --> 00:49:13.890
for many public health
schools in the United States.

00:49:13.890 --> 00:49:16.090
I must say only a handful of them

00:49:16.090 --> 00:49:18.900
have some sort of a
structured program for it.

00:49:18.900 --> 00:49:22.050
It's kind of seen as outside
of the scope of public health.

00:49:22.050 --> 00:49:23.700
So how do we train

00:49:24.600 --> 00:49:27.290
the next generation of
public health leaders

00:49:27.290 --> 00:49:29.730
to be far better prepared than we are

00:49:29.730 --> 00:49:31.620
in responding to armed conflict?

00:49:31.620 --> 00:49:33.670
That's just some question
that came to my mind.

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:35.300
And by the way I wanna ask the audience

00:49:35.300 --> 00:49:37.790
to ask put their questions in chat box.

00:49:37.790 --> 00:49:38.623
- Yeah.

00:49:38.623 --> 00:49:41.010
So Kaveh you ask a very important question

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:45.360
but I don't want to limit it
only to conflicts and wars.

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:46.790
I think one of the issues

00:49:46.790 --> 00:49:50.140
that is at the heart of public
health education and training

00:49:50.140 --> 00:49:53.290
institutionally and at
the level of individuals

00:49:53.290 --> 00:49:55.870
is the whole concept of injustice.

00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:57.980
And I don't think we do

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:02.770
the job that would expect it to do there.

00:50:02.770 --> 00:50:05.880
We wait for incidents to
happen and we react to them.

00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:08.180
We have to be proactive

00:50:08.180 --> 00:50:12.050
and start thinking about
what I'm not recommending

00:50:12.050 --> 00:50:14.530
that we only discuss politics in classes.

00:50:14.530 --> 00:50:16.560
We need to discuss (Iman
mumbles), statistics,

00:50:16.560 --> 00:50:19.637
and by the month issues
every single core discipline

00:50:19.637 --> 00:50:21.310
within public health.

00:50:21.310 --> 00:50:24.240
But the students, the faculty,

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:26.083
the institutions when they work together,

00:50:26.083 --> 00:50:29.010
they need to understand the context.

00:50:29.010 --> 00:50:30.430
What we did at the faculty,

00:50:30.430 --> 00:50:35.340
we took a bold step actually
in our vision we introduced

00:50:35.340 --> 00:50:39.330
that we're a school that
is thriving or working

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:41.320
or would like to be leaders,

00:50:41.320 --> 00:50:45.940
pushing for peace, justice,
equity, and health.

00:50:45.940 --> 00:50:50.940
We've actually put peace,
justice, and equity before health.

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:52.600
And this is out of commitment.

00:50:52.600 --> 00:50:54.830
It doesn't mean that we're able to do it,

00:50:54.830 --> 00:50:58.670
but I think schools of
public health need to realize

00:50:58.670 --> 00:51:01.230
that we need to address the core problem

00:51:01.230 --> 00:51:04.570
and start working and we need
to learn from each other.

00:51:04.570 --> 00:51:06.920
You need to instill values

00:51:07.770 --> 00:51:10.670
and sensitivity within the students

00:51:10.670 --> 00:51:12.700
in addition to the skills that they need.

00:51:12.700 --> 00:51:14.750
And also we need to build institutions.

00:51:14.750 --> 00:51:17.190
And that's a role for
a university like Yale,

00:51:17.190 --> 00:51:19.700
for the school like Yale
School of Public Health

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:21.210
and other schools here

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:23.490
that when they think
about building capacity

00:51:23.490 --> 00:51:25.430
it's not only about building the capacity

00:51:25.430 --> 00:51:27.920
of individual researchers here and there.

00:51:27.920 --> 00:51:30.330
Building the capacity of institutions

00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:31.490
because people leave.

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:34.660
I might be in (Iman mumbles)
I might be in Lebanon,

00:51:34.660 --> 00:51:35.710
I might leave Lebanon,

00:51:35.710 --> 00:51:38.640
or some one of my colleagues
might live in Lebanon.

00:51:38.640 --> 00:51:40.464
The university stays

00:51:40.464 --> 00:51:45.250
and the investment should be
at the institutional level

00:51:45.250 --> 00:51:46.980
not at only the individual level.

00:51:46.980 --> 00:51:49.770
We've introduced a certificate
and several courses

00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:51.983
on conflict within our MPH program.

00:51:53.660 --> 00:51:54.513
- Thank you.

